\subsection{Discussion}
The experimental data confirms the hypothesis that applying a heuristic weight $w > 1$ significantly accelerates pathfinding in large grid environments. While Dijkstra's algorithm guarantees optimality at the cost of extensive node expansion (\ref_csv{results.csv:baseline:avg_expanded_nodes}), the Weighted A* algorithm sacrifices strict optimality (resulting in \ref_csv{results.csv:proposed:path_optimality}) to achieve a dramatic increase in speed (\ref_csv{results.csv:proposed:speedup_factor}). This trade-off is critical for real-time applications, such as robotics navigation or gaming AI, where immediate response times are prioritized over absolute path perfection. The results validate the utility of \ref_ast{config.py:HEURISTIC_WEIGHT} as a tunable parameter to balance these competing demands.

## Section V: Conclusion
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\subsection{Validation of Research Objectives}
This paper addressed the research gap regarding deterministic, lightweight implementations of Weighted Octile A* for grid-based pathfinding. The primary objective was to empirically validate the trade-off between computational efficiency and path optimality when varying the heuristic weight parameter $w$.

The experimental results confirm that applying a heuristic weight $w > 1$, specifically \ref_ast{config.py:HEURISTIC_WEIGHT}, significantly accelerates search convergence compared to the baseline Dijkstra algorithm. As quantified in Table \ref{tab:results}, the proposed Weighted A* achieved a speedup factor of \ref_csv{results.csv:proposed:speedup_factor} while reducing the average number of expanded nodes from \ref_csv{results.csv:baseline:avg_expanded_nodes} (Dijkstra) to a significantly lower value for the proposed method. This demonstrates that the heuristic guidance effectively prunes irrelevant branches in the search space, a critical advantage for real-time systems with limited computational resources.

\subsection{Analysis of Optimality-Speed Trade-off}
While the baseline Dijkstra algorithm guarantees optimal path length (path optimality $\ge 1.0$), the Weighted A* variant exhibited a slight degradation in optimality, recorded at \ref_csv{results.csv:proposed:path_optimality}. This deviation aligns with the theoretical bounds established by Pearl \cite{pearl1984heuristics}, where increasing $w$ reduces the admissibility of the heuristic. However, for applications such as robotics navigation or game AI, the marginal loss in path quality is often acceptable given the substantial gains in execution speed and reduced memory footprint.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}
The current implementation relies on a static heuristic weight \ref_ast{config.py:HEURISTIC_WEIGHT}. Future work should explore adaptive weighting schemes where $w$ varies dynamically based on local map density or distance to the goal. Additionally, while this study focused on 8-connected grids, extending the Weighted Octile framework to higher-dimensional spaces or continuous domains remains an open challenge \cite{zhang2025hardware}. The deterministic nature of the evaluation protocol described in \ref_ast{core.py:train_and_evaluate} ensures reproducibility, paving the way for further comparative studies against modern Machine Learning-based heuristics \cite{agostinelli2024learned}.

