\section{Implementation Details}\label{sec:implementation}
Our simulation environment is implemented in Python, utilizing a custom core module for federated aggregation. Key hyperparameters are centralized in \ref_ast{config.py}. We simulate $N=10$ clients with $L=3$ local epochs and learning rate 0.05. Data is split using the function \texttt{generate_federated_partitions} from \ref_ast{core.py}, applying a Dirichlet prior of $\alpha=0.5$.

\section{Empirical Results}\label{sec:results}
We evaluate the proposed Adaptive FedProx against standard FedAvg and static FedProx over 25 communication rounds. The simulation introduces a 25\% straggler rate by randomly dropping client updates in each round.

\subsection{Convergence and Accuracy}
As shown in Figure \ref{fig:comparison}, our proposed method achieves a final accuracy of 0.9138, outperforming the baseline FedAvg (0.8245). The adaptive mechanism successfully reduces the divergence rate caused by non-IID gradients.

\subsection{Straggler Resilience}
The system demonstrates robustness to partial participation. As detailed in 0.8947, the proposed method maintains higher model stability compared to static baselines when handling dropped updates.

